well let's talk to the apprenticeship  and skills manager jillian keegan who  joins us now  good to see you this morning let's talk  about uh  a little bit of clarity on what's going  on with travel please  what is the latest  uh well actually the latest will be set  out by the transport minister on  thursday which is when the three-week  review comes  um but of course um you know what we're  trying to do is tread this careful  balance between opening up travel  but also keeping the country safe um so  i mean i know there's been a lot of  speculation in the press uh about what  uh the the  the system is going to be and obviously  we've explored many options but  effectively what we want to do is keep  it simple enough for people to really  understand  and obviously be able to take their own  decisions uh based on the system so we  have the red list countries the  uh ambalist uh countries and the green  list countries and the ambulance  countries if you're double vaccinated  you don't  have to self isolate and if you're not  double vaccinate  vaccinated than you do on your return  and in all of the systems  obviously you have to take pre-departure  tests and then a test on day two and  then there's additional tests and  additional quarantine in obviously for  red list  countries and you're only supposed to  come to redless countries if you're a  a british citizen or someone who's got a  writer residence here returning home  okay so what you seem to be indicating  there  is that amber plus is going which means  by the end of the week  presumably or the start of next week  france could be back on our travel list  well amber plus there is this one  category for france at the moment just  because of the concern over the beta  variant  but the transport secretary is next  review which is the three weekly review  he does uh every three weeks that's on  thursday this week  so he'll set out more um in terms of you  know what's happening with amber plus  and uh also with france in particular  see what what is  confusing is you say that you know  having a simplified system  is better for us because it allows us to  make a more informed choice as to what  we want to do which is exactly what  uh the minister matt warman said to me  yesterday about the idea of having  amber watch countries he said that is  providing more information it gives a  direction of travel for people to know  what is happening with a particular  country that they want to go to  and therefore they can make a better  more informed decision  you seem to be the government's rowing  back on that  well not really i mean obviously we're  exploring more options and every option  but the reality is  we're trying to tread this careful  balance and it is a careful balance  between opening up travel when we know  that there are  variants and and they're popping up in  different ways in different  countries and we know obviously there's  different case rates in different  countries as well so we are trying to be  as open as possible to travel but also  to keep people safe and that is a  difficult balance  there's no doubt it's a difficult  balance and we see countries all over  the world actually  you know putting different countries on  various lists as they all try to  navigate the balance  um but you know we're very um you know  obviously delighted with our vaccine  program  and we want to make sure that people can  benefit from that but at the same time  we are also concerned about new variants  there's no you know no doubt about that  so we're just trying to make sure that  we can  navigate that but it isn't easy to do  and  you know it's not as simple as we'd all  like as we're trying to navigate that  but you know we clearly want to open up  travel  um but also you know forgive me i mean  you're quite right and i'm sure  everybody agrees it's not simple and  it's not straightforward because nothing  has been with this pandemic but a  minister  this time yesterday telling me you know  we needed a watch list because it gives  people more clarity  today you're saying well we don't need a  watch list because it's all about giving  people  clarity and an ease of of choice  no wonder people think this is confusing  and chaotic  well maybe they better wait till the  government actually announces what it's  going to do as opposed to the  speculation that's  something i would definitely advise and  as i say the the transport secretary  will be  laying out on thursday but you know  there's no doubt that we are exploring  all options and getting that balance  between you know  giving people enough notice and warning  but also making it so that  it's simple enough it's difficult to do  you know i was out at the weekend and i  was asking people just generally did  they understand it  and it is difficult and people had  different interpretations so  you know that is a difficult balance but  i i think instead of spread  you know all speculation we will lay out  very very clearly  and of course on every three weeks the  transport secretary updates us  in terms of countries and which  countries are on which list  so this still leaves the possibility  then does it that the country could  switch  without any prior warning now if there's  going to be no watch list  could could switch from from amber to  red while you're out there so still no  guarantees of a a safe holiday if we can  put it in those terms  well obviously the transport secretary  will be setting out all of  you know the considerations on thursday  but you know i think there's no doubt  that you know we know that the pandemic  can change we know that the variance can  move we've actually  experienced that twice ourselves you  know christmas was  you know very much curtailed as we dealt  with the alpha variant and then  obviously  the general restrictions and removal of  restrictions was also delayed  as we were hit with the delta variant so  we know that these variants can hit  countries  and they can hit them in many ways what  we're really delighted with here is that  the vaccine program  looks as though it is providing a lot of  protection the case rates are coming  down we didn't know that that was going  to be the case when we  unlocked two weeks ago yesterday a lot  more of our  economy um but there's no doubt that  this  is a disease and the variance that you  know things can change very quickly  we've seen that we've seen that a number  of times ourselves  which is why the presumably the  government needs to be absolutely on the  ball with this  which raises the question what's  happening at the jbc  the the joint biosecurity center where  the the boss claire gardner  left her job without a successor being  appointed now that advises amongst other  things  on what to do about travel which  apparently what went back in june  still no appointment to replace that  that the jbc's left rudderless isn't it  i don't think so i mean these things are  you know as we've seen with many of  these committees  but it's a group of experts that are  looking at data that are giving  their analysis based on the data so you  know there's still a team of experts of  course we want to replace  uh the chair but you know there are  these team of experts that are still  looking at the data and giving their  analysis based on that  talking of confusion what about the app  i mean because  in terms of u-turns we're now told that  actually the  the um the test and trace app and the  the the way people get pinged on it is  going to be  scaled down made less sensitive  if you like which is what the government  said it wasn't going to do a couple of  weeks ago well look you know  everything's evolving i mean this is a  public health decision um so they've  been looking at the cases you know we  only  two weeks ago uh yesterday is when we  lifted a lot of restrictions in our  country as the delta case rates were  still going  up and now we've got a lot more data in  and t